<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Innu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Montagnais</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Innu, called Montagnais by the French, are an Indigenous people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> who traditionally lived by hunting caribou and fishing. Their alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> against the Iroquois, made at Tadoussac in 1603, helped found the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> on the St Lawrence. Their language belongs to the Algonquian family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/innu.jpg" alt="Innu" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Davis_Inlet_1903.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestors of the Innu lived on these lands as hunter-gatherers for many thousands of years. They call their homeland Nitassinan, meaning &quot;our land&quot;, or Innu-assi, &quot;Innu land&quot;. Innu means &quot;people&quot; in their language, Innu-aimun, which belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>–Montagnais–Naskapi dialect continuum and is unrelated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> languages of nearby peoples. The Innu are broadly divided into the Montagnais in the forested south and the Naskapi in the north, who differ in dialect and partly in way of life. Since about 1990 the Montagnais have generally chosen to be called Innu, while the Naskapi have kept the name Naskapi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Scholars do not describe the Innu as a warlike people, though they at times clashed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, the Haudenosaunee, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a>, and some of these hostilities were a side effect of European contact. Around Tadoussac they were military allies of the French in their wars with the British and their Indigenous allies, and the Haudenosaunee raided into Innu territory during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Iroquois Wars</a> of the 17th century. In June 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> set out with a party of Wendat, Algonquin and Montagnais warriors and reached the lake later named after him.</p><p>Innu oral tradition records two versions of how the French came to settle at Uepishtikueiau, which became Quebec City. In one, gifts of food and goods made the Innu dependent, and the French then turned to trade for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. In the other, more widespread, the Innu taught the French to survive, conflict followed, and the Innu, outnumbered and outarmed, withdrew from the area. In 1976 the Labrador Innu organised the Naskapi Montagnais Innu Association, renamed the Innu Nation in 1990, and they have never ceded their territory by treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>All Innu groups were traditionally nomadic. The Montagnais moved within the forested south of the peninsula, the Naskapi over the barren lands of the north. They hunted caribou in the east and north and moose in the west, and also took beaver, bear, fish, salmon and seals, and gathered berries and maple sap. They lived in wigwams covered with birchbark in the south and caribou hide in the north, and travelled by canoe in summer and by snowshoe and toboggan in winter. Women prepared the hides and made much of the clothing.</p><p>Pre-colonial belief encouraged respect for animals. The caribou was the most revered, shamans performed shaking tent rites, and hunters placed bones and skulls in trees or on raised platforms. About 28,960 people of Innu origin live in settlements and reserves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a>. At its own request the Davis Inlet community was relocated to a mainland site, Natuashish, and the Innu of Labrador won recognition as status Indians in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Innu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a first settlement at Port-Royal in Acadia in 1605, Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec in 1608 as a fur-trading post, allied himself with the <b>Montagnais</b>, Algonquin and Huron, and explored as far as Lake Huron.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Inuit traded with peoples to the south, but boundary disputes were frequent, and the Chukchi, Siberian Yupik, Gwichʼin, Cree and <b>Innu</b> kept them from moving far south of the tree line.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
